+<article id="doc">
+
+  <!-- ============ Masthead ============ -->
+  <header class="masthead">
+    <span class="eyebrow">Design document · 2026-09-04 · Proposed, reviewed (Codex: GO)</span>
+    <h1>Precomputed Shared Cog Navigation</h1>
+    <p><strong>Current memory ruling (2026-09-09).</strong> James authorized up to a fourfold increase in non-colossal memory caps. The research harness now uses a 32 MiB shared-navigation limit on non-colossal maps (twice the previous 16 MiB), with the existing 256 MiB total-navigation limit unchanged, including colossal. The largest measured configured-map shared footprint is 31,607,620 bytes; fresh memory qualification passes all 76 measured maps; timing and activation-time qualification remain incomplete. Earlier measurements and limits below describe historical designs. Timing, quality, determinism and activation requirements are unchanged. See <a href="nav-rework-evidence-2026-09-09/research/MEMORY_CAP_RULING.md">the ruling and measured rationale</a>.</p>
+    <p class="context">This document describes a rework of route planning in the Season 2 cog body. The rework replaces the per-seat, budgeted, map-sized A* with four parts: one immutable room/portal route index per map, one zone hazard overlay per episode, synchronous bounded route queries, and deterministic local steering. Implementation has not started. The repo commit is fad3029f (origin/main). Claude drafted the document for James. A Codex reviewer challenged it in three rounds, and its fact checks are included (see §10). Reference PRs #408, #409 and #410 are inputs to this design. Do not merge them.</p>
+  </header>
+
+  <!-- ============ Executive summary ============ -->
+  <section id="summary">
+    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
+    <p>Season 2 cogs stand still when they must walk. A cog with no route gets its own position as a waypoint, and thus it emits an empty movement mask. A cog that completed an older route and then gets a new goal keeps the completed route loaded. The engine reports this cog as "walking an older route", but the cog does not move. Four mechanisms cause this. Every standing order cancels the pending plan. The planner is a resumable A* over a 4 px lattice of up to 344,487 nodes, rationed at 256 work units per seat per tick. The scheduler gives no priority to seats with no route. Dead seats continue to spend budget. On giant pool maps, cold far routes take 10-21 scheduler ticks. When goals alternate for 32 ticks, the planner spends and discards every permitted unit, and 260-483 of 512 seat-ticks give no movement. Each seat also holds about 68 MB of planner workspace sized to the full lattice, which is about 2.2 GB for 32 seats.</p>
+    <p>The proposal keeps <code>BodyMap</code>, the exact collision predicate, the cost model, the follower shape and the per-seat danger field. It replaces the planner with a hierarchical route index built once per map install. The rooms and chokes from <code>BodyMap</code> become a graph of portal <em>sides</em>. Precomputed intra-room segments and per-portal crossings join the sides, and next-hop portal fields cover the inside of each room. A bounded A* over that side graph, plus bounded endpoint refinement, answers every route request in the same call, in integer fixed-point arithmetic. The query validates the route descriptor in shared scratch and then copies it atomically into about 12 KB of per-seat state. A zone hazard overlay prices paint arrival as a finite cost, never as a prune. An eight-octant steering rule moves the cog when no advancing route exists. The probe measured the shared index at about 3.5 MB on giant maps and about 16.3 MiB on colossal. The index build took 189-283 ms against a 479-499 ms <code>BodyMap</code> baseline. The gates of the task decide every estimate in this document: route cost inflation p95 ≤ 3 %, body slice p95 ≤ 4 ms, activation ≤ 2x baseline. The main accepted trade is that a static segment cannot avoid danger inside a room.</p>
+  </section>
