+  <!-- ============ 1 Problem ============ -->
+  <section id="problem">
+    <h2>1 · Problem</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li>Two verified player-visible stalls: a life with no route yet, and a finished route that survives a new goal.</li>
+      <li>Four mechanisms compound them: cancel-on-intent, a rationed lattice A*, a scheduler with no priority for route-less seats, and dead seats that keep planning.</li>
+      <li>Planner workspace is sized to the full lattice for every seat: ~68 MB per seat on a giant map, ~2.2 GB at 32 seats.</li>
+      <li>The focused test suites are green because the liveness cases are uncovered, not because they pass.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <p>The tick-by-tick body report<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-1" title="docs/reports/s2-cog-body-tick-2026-09-03.md">1</a></sup> found the symptom: Season 2 cogs stand still when they must walk. Players can see two cases. The code shows both.</p>
+
+    <p>The first case is a cog whose current life has no route. When the path length is zero, the waypoint function of the follower returns the position of the cog<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-2" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:687-693">2</a></sup>. As a result, the seat tick emits an empty movement mask and records the diagnostic state <code>bnsNoPath</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-3" title="src/shell/body.nim:1311-1330">3</a></sup>. The second case is a cog that reached the endpoint of an older route and then gets a new goal. The completed route stays loaded, and its cursor stays on the last point. That point is the position of the cog. The engine reports the state <code>bnsStalePath</code>, "walking an older route"<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-4" title="src/shell/body.nim:310">4</a></sup>, but the movement mask is zero.</p>
+
+    <h3>Four mechanisms make it worse</h3>
+    <p><strong>Order churn discards almost complete work.</strong> <code>setStandingIntent</code> cancels the pending plan on every new standing order<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-5" title="src/shell/body.nim:565-590 (cancel at :582-583)">5</a></sup>. That cancel occurs before the navigation waypoint code applies its two-cell goal hysteresis<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-6" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:726-732">6</a></sup>. Thus the hysteresis never protects a plan in flight.</p>
+    <p><strong>The planner rations its work across ticks.</strong> The planner is a resumable weighted A* on a 4 px lattice<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-7" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:15, :584-640">7</a></sup>. A pooled budget of 256 work units per configured seat per tick drives it<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-8" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:31, :572-628">8</a></sup>. On a giant pool map the lattice has 344,487 nodes. The task measured that a far route needs about 137k work units<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-9" title="Asana task 1218165906459726 and its comments (measurements, gates, decisions)">9</a></sup>. Under the share of one seat, that is hundreds of ticks.</p>
+    <p><strong>The scheduler has no priority.</strong> The round-robin over seats does not prefer seats that have no usable route<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-10" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:577-598">10</a></sup>.</p>
+    <p><strong>Dead seats continue to plan.</strong> Death only changes the <code>active</code> flag of the seat<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-11" title="src/shell/episode.nim:987-997; src/shell/body_nav.nim:305-306">11</a></sup>. The planning scheduler never reads that flag<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-12" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:572-619">12</a></sup>. Thus a dead seat can continue to spend work units that live seats need.</p>
+
+    <h3>Measurements</h3>
+    <p>The task measurements<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-9" title="Asana task 1218165906459726 and its comments (measurements, gates, decisions)">9</a></sup> come from an arm64 release build with 16 seats on battle-royale pool maps 0, 21, 42 and 63. Cold far routes complete in 10-21 scheduler ticks. When goals alternate for 32 ticks, the planner spends and discards all 131,072 permitted work units on every map. The zero-movement counts are 260/512, 286/512, 483/512 and 428/512 seat-ticks. A no-path seat at index 15 waited 6-11 ticks for its first planning visit. The peak process RSS was about 2.37 GB. The focused suites pass (<code>test_shell_body_nav</code> 20/20, <code>test_shell_body_seat</code> 22/22). Thus the liveness cases are not covered. They are not red.</p>
+
+    <h3>Why the memory is that large</h3>
+    <p>A giant map has 85,814 nav cells and 344,487 lattice nodes. <code>newBodyPlanner</code> sizes every workspace to the full lattice<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-13" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:252-270">13</a></sup>. <code>newBodyNavSystem</code> allocates one planner, one cache and one path buffer per seat<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-14" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:197-233">14</a></sup>. The table shows the per-seat total on a giant map.</p>
+
+    <table>
+      <thead>
+        <tr><th>Per-seat allocation (giant map)</th><th class="num">Size</th></tr>
+      </thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td>A* heap nodes (40 B x 344,487)</td><td class="num">13.8 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Heap positions + generations (2 x 8 B x 344,487)</td><td class="num">5.5 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Search hash tables (3 x 8 B x 1,048,576)</td><td class="num">25.2 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Keys / closed / gScore / cameFrom</td><td class="num">8.6 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Planner result path (16 B x 344,487)</td><td class="num">5.5 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Follower path buffer (16 B x 344,489)</td><td class="num">5.5 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Four route-field rasters (85,814 x 9 B each)<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-15" title="src/shell/body_cache.nim:54-64; src/shell/types.nim:441">15</a></sup></td><td class="num">3.1 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td>Danger field + visited raster (2 x 4 B x 85,814)</td><td class="num">0.7 MB</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><strong>Total per seat</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>~68 MB</strong> (x32 seats ≈ 2.2 GB)</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- ============ 2 What the design keeps ============ -->
+  <section id="keeps">
+    <h2>2 · What the design keeps from the existing code</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li><code>BodyMap</code> stays the immutable per-episode terrain layer, including rooms, chokes and goal validation.</li>
+      <li>The exact collision predicate, the cost model, the follower shape and the per-seat danger field are all retained unchanged.</li>
+      <li>The zone paint-arrival field and three reference PRs supply the hazard projection, the safety hints and the semantic target contract.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <p><strong>BodyMap.</strong> <code>BodyMap</code> is already an immutable per-episode terrain layer. It holds 1 px wall and clearance rasters (a chessboard distance transform), pixel components, and the 8 px nav grid. On that grid, <code>NavCell = 8</code>, and a cell is walkable when the clearance at its center is more than <code>PlayerHalf</code> (6 px). It computes watershed rooms over clearance. Chokes sit at room contacts, and <code>buildTopology</code> removes chokes closer than 64 px (<code>GateSeparationPx</code>)<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-16" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:13 (NavCell), :20 (GateSeparationPx), :482-658 (buildTopology)">16</a></sup>. It also holds the cover atlas, the validator tables, and the home fields. <code>validateGoal</code> is the only constructor of <code>ValidatedGoal</code><sup class="ref"><a href="#src-17" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:397-412">17</a></sup>. One convention is important for the index build. <code>roomLabelAt</code> returns a <em>one</em>-based label, but <code>BodyRoom</code> and the <code>roomA</code>/<code>roomB</code> fields of <code>BodyChoke</code> are <em>zero</em>-based indices<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-18" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:190-192 (roomLabelAt), :194-205, :633-654 (zero-based room indices)">18</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The exact collision predicate.</strong> A move is legal when <code>canStand</code> and the pixel-exact <code>segmentClear</code> both pass. The current planner tests both for every lattice step<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-19" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:164-165 (canStand); src/shell/body_planner.nim:298-329 (segmentClear), :610-617 (edge legality)">19</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The cost model.</strong> The cost of a step is its length x (1 + profileDangerWeight x danger at the midpoint of the step). When the nav cell of the midpoint is the blocked-penalty cell, the cost is multiplied by 8 (<code>FollowBlockFactor</code>). The profile danger weights are cpDefault 1.0, cpCarrier 2.5 and cpHunter 0.25<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-20" title="src/shell/body_planner.nim:18 (FollowBlockFactor), :618-628 (blocked cost), :88-92 (profile weights)">20</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The follower shape.</strong> The follower has a path buffer with a cursor. The waypoint advances when the cog is within one <code>NavCell</code>. The corridor is 20 px. The cog is stuck after 8 ticks. The blocked penalty lasts 96 ticks<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-21" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:38-43, :687-717">21</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The per-seat danger field</strong> and its rebuild, which occurs every 32 ticks with a stagger and uses at most eight visible enemies<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-22" title="src/shell/body_nav.nim:30, :44, :440-485">22</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>The zone paint-arrival field.</strong> The sim builds one <code>ZoneArrivalField</code> per episode. The field has two surfaces. The <code>damage</code> surface is not gated, and <code>zoneDamageByPaint</code> reads it. The <code>arrival</code> surface is gated by the render layer, and it can say "never" under wall art. <code>zonePaintedForDamageAt</code> compares <code>damage</code> with the elapsed clock of the schedule (<code>sim.tickCount - sim.gameStartTick</code>). The grid stride is 4 px (<code>ZoneFieldCellPx</code>). The sentinel is <code>ZoneNeverArrives = 0xFFFF</code>. <code>ensureZoneArrivalField</code> builds the field on first use<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-23" title="src/ctf/zone_field.nim:1593-1616 (two surfaces), :1762-1796 (zonePaintedForDamageAt), :65 (ZoneFieldCellPx), :263 (ZoneNeverArrives), :1644-1662 (ensureZoneArrivalField)">23</a></sup>.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>From the reference PRs.</strong> PR #408 gives four items: the conservative MIN projection of the 4 px field onto the 8 px nav grid, the finite hazard price (never a prune), the derated ETA speed of MaxSpeed x 3 / (MotionScale x 4) = 2.0625 px/tick, and the 48-tick safe-horizon bucket<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-24" title="PR #408 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/408">24</a></sup>. The top speed is 704/256 = 2.75 px/tick<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-25" title="src/ctf/sim_types.nim:514-518">25</a></sup>. PR #409 gives the fog-safe integer nav hints: ticks until paint here, ticks to safety, geodesic safe distance in px, and a retreat octant<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-26" title="PR #409 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/409">26</a></sup>. PR #410 gives <code>navigate_to</code> with <code>target_class = "zone_safe_ground"</code> and a <em>required</em> literal fallback point, which the engine resolves<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-27" title="PR #410 https://github.com/Metta-AI/coworld-ctf/pull/410">27</a></sup>.</p>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- ============ 3 Measured topology ============ -->
+  <section id="topology">
+    <h2>3 · Measured topology</h2>
+    <ul class="key-points">
+      <li>Giant pool maps have 85,814 nav cells in 43-107 rooms with 212-369 chokes. Colossal has 343,256 cells, 302 rooms and 1,237 chokes.</li>
+      <li>A probe of the proposed index shape builds in 189-283 ms on giant maps against a 479-499 ms <code>BodyMap</code> baseline, and 2,245 ms on colossal.</li>
+      <li>Every walkable cell has a room. Orthogonal cell steps are always legal. Diagonal steps are illegal 1.3-2.7 % of the time, thus a legality mask is necessary.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <p>All numbers in this section come from this machine (arm64 release build, commit fad3029f). The canonical linux/amd64 numbers come from the gate run in rollout subtask 02 (§8).</p>
+
+    <h3>Nav census</h3>
+    <p>The census comes from the <code>nav-census</code> mode of the body-port bench tool. That mode is an uncommitted diff at this time<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-28" title="tools/bench_body_port.nim nav-census (uncommitted diff of the census tool)">28</a></sup>.</p>
