+
+    <p>The index holds six kinds of data. The sizes are for pool:1 (giant) and colossal.</p>
+    <ol>
+      <li><strong>Legal moves.</strong> <code>legalMoves: seq[uint8]</code> holds one byte per nav cell. Bit <em>i</em> is set when the move to the canonical <code>Neighbors[i]</code> is legal: both cells are walkable, the move does not cut a corner, and pixel <code>segmentClear</code> passes between the centers. This is the <em>one</em> legality oracle for the index build, the smoothing of the follower, the local search and the steering. <code>segmentClear</code> and <code>Neighbors</code> move into the body map module, so that there is exactly one implementation. Today the map and the planner each have a copy<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-31" title="src/shell/body_map.nim:24-27; src/shell/body_planner.nim:20-22, :298 (duplicated Neighbors and segmentClear)">31</a></sup>. Size: 86 KB giant / 343 KB colossal.</li>
+      <li><strong>Room compaction.</strong> <code>roomOf: seq[uint16]</code> holds the zero-based room index per nav cell. <code>roomCellStart</code> and <code>roomCells</code> hold the cell indices per room in row-major order. <code>localIndex: seq[int32]</code> holds the index of each nav cell inside its room. The build asserts that every walkable cell has a room. The probe found that this is always true on 8 maps (§3). Size: about 0.6 MB / 2.4 MB.</li>
+      <li><strong>Portals.</strong> Portals are the <code>BodyMap</code> chokes, with two sides. For each choke, the build stores an anchor cell on the roomA side and one on the roomB side. Each anchor is the nearest walkable cell of that room to the choke pixel, found within a bounded ring in a fixed ring order. The build also stores the legal crossing between the two anchors. The crossing is a direct legal edge, or a bounded build-time search whose cells become a crossing segment. Activation asserts that every portal reaches both rooms. The one-sided rule of the probe gave 0-12 anchor mismatches per map (§3).</li>
+      <li><strong>Portal fields.</strong> For each (room <em>r</em>, portal <em>p</em> of <em>r</em>), the index holds one <code>uint8</code> next-hop per cell of <em>r</em>. The next-hop is the index of the legal neighbor toward the anchor of <em>p</em> on the side of <em>r</em>. One room-restricted Dijkstra computes it, and ties go to <code>Neighbors</code> order. The index does not store distances. A query that needs the distance from a cell to <em>p</em> walks the chain. Exact scoring walks the chain in any case. The measured maximum chain is 213 cells on giant and 436 on colossal. The Dijkstra uses one transient <code>uint32</code> distance array over one room, and the build reuses it. Size: 1.7 MB / 9.5 MB.</li>
+      <li><strong>Intra-room segments.</strong> For every portal pair (<em>p</em> &lt; <em>q</em>) of a room, the index holds the cell path <em>p</em> → <em>q</em>, read from the Dijkstra parents of <em>p</em>. The path is flat (<code>segmentCells: seq[int32]</code> with start and length), with the static length as <code>uint32</code> in 1/16 px and a bounding box. Pairs that are not reachable inside the room get no segment (measured 1-5 % of pairs). The abstract graph routes around them through other rooms. Size: 0.9 MB / 3.1 MB.</li>
+      <li><strong>Portal-side graph.</strong> The nodes are the (portal, incident room) <em>sides</em>, at most 2,474 on colossal. The edges are the intra-room segments between sides of the same room (at most 27,460 directed) plus one crossing edge per portal between its two sides (at most 2,474 directed). Because the crossing is an edge, a room change always pays the length, danger, blocked and hazard cost of the crossing. Every selected edge names exactly one segment.</li>
+    </ol>
+
+    <p><strong>Budget.</strong> The retained total is about 3.5 MB on giant (limit 16 MiB) and about 16.3 MiB on colossal (limit 32 MiB). The transient build workspace is one heap and one distance array over one room (at most 70,041 cells), and the build reuses it. The build time of the probe is an upper bound for this shape. It was 189-283 ms on giant maps against a 479-499 ms <code>BodyMap</code> baseline (total 1.4-1.6x the baseline, cap 2x). It was 2,245 ms on colossal against 2,263 ms (total 2.0x, cap 3x)<sup class="ref"><a href="#src-29" title="scratch seg_probe.nim (this session)">29</a></sup>. The canonical linux/amd64 numbers come from the gate run of subtask 02.</p>
